---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/containers.json';
import pallets from '../data/pallets.json';
import trailers from '../data/trailers.json';
import dw from '../data/dimweight.json';
---
<Base title="How We Calculate" description="The load planning method, the checks every plan passes, and the sources behind every figure on Load Plan Calc.">
  <section class="prose">
    <h1>How we calculate</h1>
    <p class="lede">Every plan is built in your browser by the same engine, checked independently before it is shown, and based on figures we can link to.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose">
    <h2>The method</h2>
    <p>Load planning — fitting boxes into a larger box — is a hard optimisation problem with no fast method that is always optimal. We use block building, a well-studied heuristic for container loading (Eley, M. (2002) “Solving container loading problems by block arrangement”, European Journal of Operational Research 141; Fanslau, T. and Bortfeldt, A. (2010) “A tree search algorithm for solving the container loading problem”, INFORMS Journal on Computing 22). The container is filled from the back wall, one block of identical boxes at a time, with a look-ahead that scores each block by how well the space left over can still be filled. Many variants are run and the plan that loads the most volume is kept.</p>
    <p>In semi-automatic mode you set the loading steps (box type, quantity, an optional fixed position, and whether a step starts a new section). The same search runs for each step in turn, starting from the space the earlier steps left, so the steps keep your order while each one is packed as tightly as the method allows.</p>
    <p>When you edit a plan by hand, each move, turn or new box is checked against the rules below before it is made. The free spots offered for a box are the corners next to, beside, behind and on top of the boxes already loaded (the “extreme points” of Crainic, T.G., Perboli, G. and Tadei, R. (2008) “Extreme point-based heuristics for three-dimensional bin packing”, INFORMS Journal on Computing 20) that pass every check. “Fill the rest automatically” keeps your boxes, fills the gaps among them block by block from those corner points, and loads the empty space in front of them with the block-building method.</p>
    <p>On a pallet, the load space is the pallet's footprint plus the overhang you allow on each side, and the height limit minus the pallet's height. With one box type, the planner first plans a full pallet (as many boxes as fit, without the weight limit) and then keeps its lowest boxes, layer by layer, up to your quantity and the max load weight. Each box in such a plan stands on boxes placed before it, so it stays a valid plan, and it passes the same checks. Ti-Hi (boxes per layer × layers) is read from the layers of that full pallet, and only shown when every box stands with the same side up on a clean layer line.</p>
    <p>Whole pallets in a container are planned as boxes: the footprint of the pallet, the loaded height and the gross weight, kept “this side up”. The counts in the table on <a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-container/">how many pallets fit in a container</a> are computed by the engine when the site is built and checked by the same validator; the build stops if a plan fails the check. The same goes for the table on <a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-53-foot-trailer/">how many pallets fit in a 53 ft trailer</a>, where the load space is a 53' dry van's inside length, width and height as the trailer maker prints them in inches, converted exactly to millimetres.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/cbm-calculator/">CBM calculator</a> is plain arithmetic: each carton's length × width × height in metres, times its quantity, summed; cubic feet = cubic metres ÷ 0.028316846592 (0.3048³). The share of a container is the total divided by the container's inside length × width × height. Because cartons leave gaps, that share is an upper bound; the table of example cartons on that page sets it against counts the engine computes at build time and the validator checks.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/dimensional-weight-calculator/">dimensional weight calculator</a> follows the steps of the USPS Domestic Mail Manual as written: each side converted to inches (exactly) and rounded up to a whole inch (rounded off where the DMM says so), length × width × height (× 0.785 for a package that is not rectangular), and only above 1,728 in³ divided by 139 (166 in the commercial Priority Mail nonrectangular text) and rounded up to a whole pound; the billable weight is the greater of that and the actual weight. Integer arithmetic is used for the rounding steps so no floating-point error can move a result across a whole number. The table of box sizes on that page is computed with the same code at build time and checked against the hand formula. With your own divisor, nothing is rounded.</p>
    <p>Because it is a heuristic, a plan may occasionally fit a few boxes fewer than the best possible arrangement. It will never show an arrangement that is physically impossible: the checks below make sure of that.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Checks on every plan</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>No two boxes overlap, and every box is inside the load space.</li>
      <li>Each box is stowed in one of its allowed orientations (all six, “this side up”, or the positions you ticked); “this side up” boxes keep their height vertical.</li>
      <li>Semi-automatic plans: each step holds only its own box type, no more than asked, in its fixed position if one was chosen, and a step that starts a new section lies wholly in front of earlier cargo.</li>
      <li>In a container, each box fits through the door opening in the way it is stowed. On a pallet, no box sticks out past the pallet edge plus the overhang you allow, or above the height limit.</li>
      <li>Every box stands fully on the floor or on boxes below it, and nothing stands on a box marked as not stackable.</li>
      <li>The total weight is within the limit you entered, and no more boxes are placed than you entered.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The engine's test suite also checks hand-calculated cases (for example, 8 cubes of 500 mm in a 1 m cube, and 660 cartons of 600 × 400 × 400 mm in a 40' high cube), 40 random cases, a fixed-position case (600 cartons when the 600 × 400 × 400 mm carton may only stand one way) 30 random semi-automatic plans, and hand-editing cases: the placement rules, pushing and free spots on small hand-checked layouts, refilling the 660-carton plan after boxes are taken out of the middle (back to 660), and 25 random plans with boxes removed and filled again. Pallet cases: 48 cartons of 400 × 300 × 250 mm on a Euro pallet with a 1,800 mm limit (8 × 6), the weight-limited 48 × 40 in case (16 × 5 + 13 = 93), partial quantities built layer by layer, and 20 random pallets. Pallets in containers: Euro pallets one high in a 20', 40' and 45' (11, 25 and 27 by hand: two lanes, crosswise and lengthwise), two high in a 40' (50), 48 × 40 in pallets in a 40' high cube (20 one high, 40 two high at 50 in, but 20 in a 40' Standard where two would not fit under the roof), half pallets in a 20' (25), a loaded pallet taller than the door (0), and a payload-limited case (23 pallets of 1,200 kg). Pallets in a 53' trailer (630 × 101.5 × 110 in inside): 48 × 40 in pallets 30 one high (two across with the 48 in side across, 15 rows of 40 in), 26 when the 48 in side must run along the trailer, 60 two high at 50 in, and Euro pallets 40 (two crosswise, 20 rows of 800 mm), also in the 98.5 in wide model.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Units</h2>
    <p>Everything is calculated in millimetres and kilograms. Inches and pounds use the exact definitions 1 in = 25.4 mm and 1 lb = 0.45359237 kg; volumes use 1 ft = 0.3048 m (1 ft³ = 0.028316846592 m³, 1 in³ = 16.387064 cm³).</p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <div class="tablebox"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Figure</th><th>Source</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.containers.map((c) => <tr><td>{c.name}: inside size, door, tare, payload, gross</td><td><a href={c.url} rel="noopener">Hapag-Lloyd</a></td><td>{data.source.checked}</td></tr>)}
        {pallets.pallets.map((x) => <tr><td>{x.name}: footprint, height, weight, safe working load, stacking limit</td><td><a href={x.url} rel="noopener">{x.publisher}</a></td><td>{pallets.checked}</td></tr>)}
        {trailers.trailers.map((t) => <tr><td>{t.name}: interior length, width, height; interior volume; GVWR</td><td><a href={t.url} rel="noopener">{trailers.source.publisher}</a></td><td>{trailers.source.checked}</td></tr>)}
        {dw.rules.flatMap((r) => r.sections).map((s) => <tr><td>USPS dimensional weight rule ({s.service}): 1,728 in³ threshold, divisor, 0.785 factor, rounding, 70-pound price</td><td><a href={s.url} rel="noopener">USPS {s.ref}</a></td><td>{dw.source.checked}</td></tr>)}
        <tr><td>USPS definitions of round up, round off, round down</td><td><a href={dw.refs.round.url} rel="noopener">USPS {dw.refs.round.ref}</a></td><td>{dw.source.checked}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USPS weight increments (Priority Mail retail; Ground Advantage retail) and the 70-pound Priority Mail limit</td><td><a href={dw.refs.pmIncrement.url} rel="noopener">USPS DMM 123 1.1, 2.1</a>; <a href={dw.refs.gaIncrement.url} rel="noopener">DMM 133 1.2</a></td><td>{dw.source.checked}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Definition of GVWR (gross vehicle weight rating)</td><td><a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-V/part-571/subpart-A/section-571.3" rel="noopener">49 CFR 571.3 (eCFR)</a></td><td>2026-10-21</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Packing, securing and weight distribution guidance</td><td><a href="https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/safety/pages/ctu-code.aspx" rel="noopener">IMO/ILO/UNECE CTU Code</a></td><td>2026-10-18</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>Found an error? <a href="/contact/">Tell us</a> — we check and correct the figures regularly.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
